\section{Architecture critique}

Each decision below gets the same treatment: what it is, what it buys, what
it costs, real alternatives, and a verdict. ``Replace'' appears rarely ---
most of this architecture is earning its keep.

\subsection{D1 --- One 20k-line HTML file, vanilla JS, no build step}

\textbf{Pros.} Deploy is \texttt{aws s3 cp} with a hash check; zero
dependency churn or supply-chain surface; everything greppable in one file;
the test harness can extract and execute real source; no framework
abandonment risk; instant cold load (one fetch).

\textbf{Cons.} No module system, so isolation is by naming convention
(\texttt{\_coeffProgram*}) and load-order/TDZ guards that look like noise
until removed; 774 functions share one namespace; no static analysis ---
the serializer self-recursion shipped because nothing type-checks or
lint-checks the file; editing is index-arithmetic at 20k lines; review
diffs concentrate in one path.

\textbf{Alternatives.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=1pt]
  \item \emph{ES modules, still no bundler.} Split into
  \texttt{js/*.mjs} loaded via \texttt{<script type="module">}; S3 serves
  them fine. Real imports kill the TDZ-guard idiom and give the harness
  import-level seams. Cost: a deliberate state-object pass (the implicit
  global state must become explicit), cache-busting on
  uploads. Medium effort, permanent payoff.
  \item \emph{Keep one file, add checking.} JSDoc type annotations +
  \texttt{tsc --checkJs} + ESLint (\texttt{no-self-compare},
  recursion and shadow rules) wired into \texttt{predeploy\_check.sh}.
  Catches the bug classes actually observed. Days, not weeks.
  \item \emph{A framework (React/Svelte/Vue).} Componentized chips and
  panels, but imports a build pipeline, a dependency treadmill, and a full
  state-management rewrite into a codebase whose value is determinism.
  Weakest option here.
\end{enumerate}

\verdict{Keep the buildless philosophy; do alternative 2 now and treat
alternative 1 as the next structural step. Skip 3.}

\subsection{D2 --- bash deploy.sh as the infrastructure layer}

\textbf{Pros.} Readable end to end; no opaque CloudFormation state or drift
mystery; converge semantics (create falls through to update and vice
versa); \texttt{show-build} verifies deployed-vs-local instead of assuming;
after the CR5 unification there is exactly one Lambda spec list, structure
pinned by tests.

\textbf{Cons.} 2{,}144 lines of bash is a bespoke platform: no plan/diff
before apply, no changesets or rollback, IAM hand-rolled, no second
environment without hand-cloning names/buckets; failure mid-deploy leaves a
partially-updated stack (mitigated, not solved, by idempotence).

\textbf{Alternatives.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=1pt]
  \item \emph{Terraform/CDK/SAM wholesale.} Plan-before-apply and
  environments for free, but you trade a transparent script for state files
  and abstractions, re-encode 41 functions + 3 ASL templates + IAM, and
  lose the inline gates. High cost, moderate payoff at this team size.
  \item \emph{Manifest-driven bash.} Keep the engine, move the data out:
  the spec list, routes, and env maps become one JSON (the repo already has
  \texttt{api\_manifest.json} as a half-step); deploy.sh iterates it, tests
  validate it, and the same JSON could later feed any IaC tool. Low cost,
  kills remaining list-drift classes.
  \item \emph{Hybrid:} Terraform for the slow-moving substrate (bucket,
  table, roles, API), script for the fast-moving Lambda/ASL layer.
\end{enumerate}

\verdict{Keep, with alternative 2. Revisit IaC only when a second
environment or a second operator becomes real.}

\subsection{D3 --- 41 single-purpose Lambdas, shared code copied into each zip}

\textbf{Pros.} Per-function memory/CPU tuning (10{,}240\,MB solvers next to
512\,MB metadata routes --- this is real money); blast-radius isolation;
reserved concurrency protects storage/dispatch from render storms;
per-function logs.

\textbf{Cons.} 41 zips $\times$ (handler, deps, env, layers, tmp, IAM,
route, permission) is wiring that must be tested to stay true ---
the packaging tests exist \emph{because} the surface is wide;
\texttt{shared.py} and friends are \emph{copied} into every zip, so two
Lambdas can run different versions of the same module mid-deploy; 41 cold
starts; per-zip 250\,MB limits force the layer dance for libvips/LAPACK.

\textbf{Alternatives.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=1pt]
  \item \emph{Shared-code layer.} One \texttt{polypaint-common} layer for
  the pure-Python helpers; zips shrink to handler + binaries; version skew
  window collapses to the layer publish. Low effort; the layer mechanism is
  already in daily use.
  \item \emph{One container image, many handlers.} A single ECR image with
  every handler and binary; each function differs only by \texttt{CMD}.
  Kills zip drift entirely, slower publishes, 10\,GB budget. Medium effort.
  \item \emph{Consolidation into role-based Lambdas} (sync API, async
  worker, orchestration): fewer moving parts but loses per-function tuning
  --- the 10\,GB/512\,MB split is worth keeping.
  \item \emph{Fargate for the long renders}: removes the 15-minute ceiling
  if it ever binds; not currently binding.
\end{enumerate}

\verdict{Keep the topology; adopt alternative 1 (shared layer) --- it is
the cheapest fix for the realest risk (version skew).}

\subsection{D4 --- Python handlers around native C binaries via JSON-over-stdin}

\textbf{Pros.} The solver runs at native speed with LAPACK where it counts;
process isolation means a solver crash is a clean Python exception, not a
corrupted runtime; the JSON protocol is observable (any spec replays from a
shell); Python stays in charge of S3/DynamoDB where it belongs.

\textbf{Cons.} Cross-language duplication is structural: opcodes, limits,
selector enums, and registry ids exist in Python, C, and JS, held together
by drift tests (a tax, paid correctly, but a tax); process spawn +
serialization per invocation; builds require a Docker round-trip inside
deploy; binary staleness is a real failure mode (hence the mtime gate).

\textbf{Alternatives.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=1pt]
  \item \emph{Generate the mirrors.} One \texttt{vm\_spec.json} (opcodes,
  limits, fn indices, aliases) from which a small generator emits the C
  header, the Python constants, and the JS tables ---
  \texttt{gen\_catalog} already proves the pattern in this repo. Drift
  tests then verify generated output instead of hand-copies. This is the
  highest-leverage architecture change available.
  \item \emph{In-process via cffi/ctypes.} Load the VM as a shared library:
  no spawn, no JSON hop. Loses crash isolation and complicates the layer
  story; only worth it if per-call overhead ever shows in profiles.
  \item \emph{Rust + PyO3.} One toolchain, memory safety, good cross-compile.
  A future, not a refactor: 10k lines of validated C with 40 WASM-parity
  tests is an asset, not a liability.
\end{enumerate}

\verdict{Keep the split; do alternative 1. Treat 2 and 3 as
profile-driven, not aesthetic, decisions.}

\subsection{D5 --- Two stack-machine DSLs with text and chip front ends}

\textbf{Pros.} Fingerprintable, deterministic, bounded programs (no
\texttt{eval}, no user JS in Lambdas); text mode for power, chips for
discoverability, one compiler under both; macros enable composition; the
typed stack + scalar-expression bytecode split cleanly separates stack
values from per-row arguments (now documented as design).

\textbf{Cons.} Four representations of one program (text, chip rows, binary
tokens, JS catalog) and \emph{two} sibling-but-different VMs (param vs
coeff) multiply every concept; the recent review cycle (CR2, CR6, CR7) was
dominated by mirror-sync bugs --- aliases, canonical names, src/tgt order;
chips serialize wire rows rather than user intent, which leaks all the way
into the UI (Section~4).

\textbf{Alternatives.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=1pt]
  \item \emph{Single catalog source of truth} (same move as D4-1): chip
  defs, aliases, param shapes, andy arity, selector vocab in one JSON; JS
  catalog and Python tables generated. Eliminates the CR6/CR7 bug class
  rather than patching instances of it.
  \item \emph{Unify the two VMs}: one token VM with two register files
  (param scalars, coeff vectors). Conceptually right, but fingerprints are
  wire format --- this is a migration with versioned specs, not a refactor.
  Queue behind a real feature need.
  \item \emph{Adopt an existing language} (Lua, a JS subset): more
  expressive, but forfeits determinism, fingerprint stability, and the
  bounded execution that makes Lambda-side eval safe. Wrong trade here.
  \item \emph{Make text the only source} and render chips as a read-only
  visualization: halves the editing surface; viable only after the UI in
  Section~5.2 exists.
\end{enumerate}

\verdict{Keep the DSL design; invest in alternative 1 immediately, hold 2
until a feature forces a fingerprint version bump anyway.}

\subsection{D6 --- Fingerprint-addressed artifact cache, frozen wire formats}

\textbf{Pros.} Identical work is never recomputed; cache keys are
content-derived so correctness does not depend on invalidation logic;
byte-stability discipline made several refactors provably safe (verified
against HEAD during the cleanup pass).

\textbf{Cons.} The freeze constrains internals forever (the
\texttt{\_execution\_spec} printer cannot change cosmetically); a
historical quirk --- the src/tgt order flip between row layouts --- is now
permanent; nothing in the key encodes \emph{format generation}, so any
future change is all-or-nothing.

\textbf{Alternatives.} Add a \texttt{spec\_version} field folded into the
fingerprint: old artifacts stay valid under v1 while v2 can fix layout
quirks; dual-read during transition. Cheap insurance, no behavior change
today.

\verdict{Keep; add the version field at the next natural migration.}

\subsection{D7 --- Step Functions + dispatch Lambda + DynamoDB status + UI polling}

\textbf{Pros.} Standard workflows give visible execution history, retries,
and state-size discipline; DynamoDB rows with TTL are a clean progress
ledger; the dispatch Lambda centralizes async fan-out targets in one env
map (single spec list, post-CR5).

\textbf{Cons.} ASL templating via \texttt{sed} substitution in deploy is
stringly-typed (mitigated by shared renderers + definition tests);
payload-contract drift across planner/ASL/worker is the checklist's
heaviest section for a reason; the UI polls --- status latency and request
noise --- and shows text, not the DAG that Step Functions already knows.

\textbf{Alternatives.} (1) WebSocket API or IoT-Core topic pushing status
deltas: kills polling, enables the jobs rail in Section~5.3; medium effort.
(2) EventBridge-driven choreography instead of orchestration: more
decoupled, much harder to see; not recommended --- visibility is this
system's strength. (3) Surface the existing SFN execution history directly
in the UI (read-only API call) before building anything new.

\verdict{Keep; do alternative 3 cheaply, then 1 when the jobs rail lands.}

\subsection{D8 --- The verification stack}

\textbf{Pros.} This is the standout: enum/limit drift tests across three
languages; packaging tests that parse deploy.sh; payload-shape contract
tests; a Docker gate that runs the real ARM binaries; a frontend harness
that now \emph{executes} extracted source (the recursion bug forced that
upgrade); fingerprint-stability checks against HEAD during refactors.

\textbf{Cons.} The JS harness is homegrown extract-and-pin --- exact-text
pins mean edits to pinned lines must update the harness in the same change
(a feature for wire format, friction elsewhere); no browser-level E2E, so
DOM wiring (onclick strings, element ids) is tested only by convention;
property-based testing is absent where it would shine (random programs:
Python fold vs C eval parity).

\textbf{Alternatives.} A 10-case Playwright smoke (load, add chip, compute
preview, render, fetch result) covering the DOM seam; Hypothesis-based
program generators for VM parity; after an ES-module split, real unit
imports replace source extraction.

\verdict{Keep and extend: Playwright smoke + property tests are the two
missing legs.}

\subsection{Decision matrix}

<<matrix, results='asis'>>=
m <- data.frame(
  Decision = c("D1 buildless single-file UI", "D2 bash deployer",
               "D3 41 small Lambdas", "D4 C core via subprocess",
               "D5 two DSLs, four mirrors", "D6 fingerprint cache",
               "D7 SFN + polling", "D8 verification stack"),
  Call = c("Keep + add checking", "Keep + manifest-drive",
           "Keep + shared layer", "Keep + generate mirrors",
           "Keep + one catalog", "Keep + spec version",
           "Keep + push status", "Keep + E2E/property"),
  Risk.if.ignored = c("more unexecuted-path bugs", "second-env pain",
                      "mid-deploy version skew", "three-way drift tax grows",
                      "next CR6-class bug", "frozen quirks accumulate",
                      "polling UX ceiling", "DOM-seam regressions")
)
names(m) <- c("Decision", "Call", "Risk if ignored")
knitr::kable(m, format = "latex", booktabs = TRUE, align = "lll", linesep = "")
@

