\subsection{What works}

Density without navigation depth --- every control is one click away;
chips render as readable formulas, not opaque IDs; the text editor with a
real compiler and diagnostics is a power feature most tools never get;
dark theme and accent discipline are consistent; nothing lies --- status
text reflects the actual contract.

\subsection{Why it feels constraining}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=2pt]
  \item \textbf{The layout mirrors the wire format, not the workflow.} Each
  row is a payload field. The loop you actually run (edit $\to$ preview
  $\to$ render $\to$ compare) is spread across Compute, Render, and Results
  tabs that cannot be seen together.
  \item \textbf{Stack programs in a flat strip.} RPN chips hide the one
  thing that matters --- stack depth and what each chip consumes/produces.
  You simulate the machine in your head; the compiler knows but the UI
  doesn't show it.
  \item \textbf{Editing in 12-character boxes.} Scalar expressions like
  \texttt{log(abs(p1+p2)+1)*1j} are edited in tiny inline inputs; the
  \texttt{*Wide} CSS classes are band-aids on a structural problem (no
  inspector surface).
  \item \textbf{Binary Chips/Text toggle.} The two views never coexist, so
  text users lose chip affordances and chip users never learn the text
  idiom. Param Program doesn't get a text mode at all --- an asymmetry with
  no user-facing reason.
  \item \textbf{Catalog browsing by native \texttt{<select>}.} 28 coeff
  transforms in a dropdown; pickers are popup lists without search-as-you-
  type, previews, or examples.
  \item \textbf{Jobs are log lines.} Long pipelines report through appended
  text; there is no persistent surface answering ``what is running, how far
  along, what did it produce'' --- despite the backend knowing exactly that.
  \item \textbf{No direct manipulation of parameters.} \texttt{p1/p2/t1/t2}
  exploration is type-a-number-and-recompute, although a lores preview
  Lambda exists that could make dragging live.
  \item \textbf{Fixed geometry.} No panel resize, no collapse memory beyond
  preview tabs, no layout choice for wide monitors.
\end{enumerate}

\section{UI alternatives}

Ordered roughly by effort. A--D keep the existing DOM and grow it; E--F are
structural.

\subsection{A --- Command palette + chip inspector (days)}

Two additive features. \texttt{Ctrl-K} opens a fuzzy palette over
\emph{every} action and catalog entry (add chip, switch tab, run compute,
open saved program). Clicking any chip opens a right-side inspector with
full-width labeled fields, the chip's doc line, and stack effect --- the
tiny inline boxes stay for glanceability but stop being the only editor.

<<mockA, fig.width=6.9, fig.height=3.4>>=
wf_new()
wf_box(2, 2, 60, 92, "", fill = "#FBFBFD")
wf_note(4, 90, "Compute tab as today...", cex = 0.52)
x <- 4; x <- wf_chip(x, 80, "cf"); x <- wf_chip(x, 80, "littlewood(0,1)")
xsel <- x; x <- wf_chip(x, 80, "linear(poly,2,3)", fill = "#FFE3E9"); x <- wf_chip(x, 80, "emit")
wf_note(4, 74, "selected chip highlighted", cex = 0.47, col = ACC)
wf_box(14, 30, 40, 26, "", fill = "#FFFFFF", border = ACC, lwd = 1.6)
text(34, 52.5, "Ctrl-K  command palette", cex = 0.56, font = 2, col = INK)
wf_box(16, 44.5, 36, 4.5, "> lin", fill = "#F2F3F8", cex = 0.5, align = "l")
wf_note(17, 41.0, "linear(src, a, b)        coeff chip", cex = 0.48)
wf_note(17, 38.0, "linspace(start,stop,n)   constructor", cex = 0.48)
wf_note(17, 35.0, "Copy legacy transforms   action", cex = 0.48)
wf_note(17, 32.0, "Render: generate MT      action", cex = 0.48)
wf_box(64, 2, 34, 92, "", fill = "#FFFFFF", border = ACC, lwd = 1.6)
text(81, 90, "Inspector: linear", cex = 0.6, font = 2, col = INK)
wf_note(66, 84.5, "linear(src, multiplier, offset[, andy])", cex = 0.5)
wf_note(66, 80.5, "src*a + b, elementwise", cex = 0.5)
wf_box(66, 70, 30, 6, " src        [poly        v]", cex = 0.5, align = "l", fill = "#F7F7FB")
wf_box(66, 62, 30, 6, " multiplier [2                ]", cex = 0.5, align = "l", fill = "#F7F7FB")
wf_box(66, 54, 30, 6, " offset     [3                ]", cex = 0.5, align = "l", fill = "#F7F7FB")
wf_box(66, 46, 30, 6, " andy       [0       ] blend", cex = 0.5, align = "l", fill = "#F7F7FB")
wf_note(66, 38, "stack: consumes 0, writes poly", cex = 0.5)
wf_note(66, 34, "docs: scale/shift shorthands exist", cex = 0.5)
wf_note(66, 28, "full-width fields end the 12-char box era", cex = 0.5, col = ACC)
@

\textbf{Cost/benefit.} No layout change, no backend change; the inspector
reuses the existing catalog metadata verbatim. Highest
value-per-line-of-code available.

\subsection{B --- Unified program editor: text and chips side by side (1--2 weeks)}

Kill the mode toggle. One pane shows text, the other chips; both are views
of the same parsed program, edits in either round-trip live through the
existing compiler. A gutter shows per-statement stack depth (the compiler
already computes \texttt{stack\_max}); errors annotate the offending line
\emph{and} chip. Param Program gets text mode for free since it shares the
machinery.

<<mockB, fig.width=6.9, fig.height=3.3>>=
wf_new()
wf_box(2, 2, 96, 92, "", fill = "#FBFBFD")
text(4, 90, "Coeff Program", cex = 0.6, font = 2, col = INK, adj = c(0,0.5))
wf_box(4, 10, 44, 74, "", fill = "#1B1B26")
wf_note(6, 80, "1  cf                      | 1", col = "#D8D8E4", cex = 0.52)
wf_note(6, 75, "2  littlewood(0, 1)        | 2", col = "#D8D8E4", cex = 0.52)
wf_note(6, 70, "3  blend(0.25)             | 1", col = "#D8D8E4", cex = 0.52)
wf_note(6, 65, "4  linear(pop, 2, 3)       | 1", col = "#D8D8E4", cex = 0.52)
wf_note(6, 60, "5  emit                    | 0", col = "#D8D8E4", cex = 0.52)
wf_note(6, 52, "6  emit", col = "#FF8AA0", cex = 0.52)
wf_note(6, 46, "   ^ stack is empty here", col = "#FF8AA0", cex = 0.5)
wf_note(33, 86.8, "stack depth gutter", col = ACC, cex = 0.48)
wf_box(50, 10, 46, 74, "", fill = "#FFFFFF")
y <- 76
lab <- c("cf", "littlewood(0,1)", "blend(0.25)", "linear(pop,2,3)", "emit")
dep <- c("d1","d2","d1","d1","d0")
for (i in seq_along(lab)) {
  wf_chip(52, y, lab[i], w = 26)
  wf_note(80, y + 2.5, dep[i], cex = 0.48, col = "#7A86A8")
  if (i < length(lab)) wf_arrow(65, y, 65, y - 2.6, lwd = 0.9)
  y <- y - 7.6
}
wf_box(52, y, 26, 5, "", fill = "#FFE3E9", border = ACC)
text(65, y + 2.5, "emit  (error)", cex = 0.5, col = ACC, family = "mono")
wf_note(50, 6, "same AST, two live views; selecting either side highlights both", cex = 0.52, col = ACC)
@

\textbf{Cost/benefit.} Pure frontend; the compiler endpoints exist
(\texttt{/compile-coeff-program-source} round-trips today). Makes the stack
visible, ends the toggle, and converts the text editor from expert escape
hatch into the primary surface with training wheels.

\subsection{C --- Jobs rail: the pipeline as a first-class surface (1 week)}

A persistent bottom strip across all tabs: one card per job showing the
compute$\to$render$\to$finalize stages with live per-stage state from the
existing status rows (later, pushed over WebSocket). Click a stage for its
log slice and outputs; click the artifact to jump to Results with it
selected. The log lines remain, demoted to a detail view.

<<mockC, fig.width=6.9, fig.height=2.6>>=
wf_new()
wf_box(2, 40, 96, 54, "", fill = "#FBFBFD")
wf_note(4, 88, "any tab content above...", cex = 0.52)
wf_box(2, 2, 96, 34, "", fill = "#FFFFFF", border = ACC, lwd = 1.5)
text(4, 31.5, "Jobs", cex = 0.58, font = 2, col = INK, adj = c(0,0.5))
wf_box(4, 8, 28, 19, "", fill = "#F7F7FB")
wf_note(5.5, 23.5, "job 7f3a  'littlewood sweep'", cex = 0.5)
wf_box(6, 11, 7, 7, "plan\ndone", fill = "#DDF2E4", cex = 0.42)
wf_box(14, 11, 7, 7, "chunks\n31/40", fill = "#FFF3D6", cex = 0.42)
wf_box(22, 11, 7, 7, "final\n--", fill = "#EFEFF4", cex = 0.42)
wf_arrow(13, 14.5, 14, 14.5, lwd = 0.9); wf_arrow(21, 14.5, 22, 14.5, lwd = 0.9)
wf_box(36, 8, 28, 19, "", fill = "#F7F7FB")
wf_note(37.5, 23.5, "job 9c01  render 12000px", cex = 0.5)
wf_box(38, 11, 7, 7, "raster\ndone", fill = "#DDF2E4", cex = 0.42)
wf_box(46, 11, 7, 7, "merge\ndone", fill = "#DDF2E4", cex = 0.42)
wf_box(54, 11, 7, 7, "finalize\nrun 102s", fill = "#FFF3D6", cex = 0.42)
wf_arrow(45, 14.5, 46, 14.5, lwd = 0.9); wf_arrow(53, 14.5, 54, 14.5, lwd = 0.9)
wf_box(68, 8, 28, 19, "", fill = "#F7F7FB")
wf_note(69.5, 23.5, "job a2b8  palette x256", cex = 0.5)
wf_box(70, 11, 7, 7, "FAILED", fill = "#FFE3E9", cex = 0.42, border = ACC)
wf_note(78.5, 14.5, "chunk 3: vector len 257 > 256\n[retry] [open log]", cex = 0.42)
@

\textbf{Cost/benefit.} Frontend + one read endpoint reshuffle; the data
already exists in DynamoDB and SFN history. This single feature addresses
the loudest ``constraining'' complaint --- losing sight of work in flight.

\subsection{D --- Direct manipulation: XY pad + draggable markers (1 week)}

Bind a 2-D pad to \texttt{(p1, p2)} with the grid extent drawn on it; drag
to scrub --- each move debounces into the deployed lores-preview Lambda and
paints the small canvas; release to commit values back into the form.
Scrubbing \emph{is} exploration; the current sweep grid becomes a window
you position by feel.

<<mockD, fig.width=6.9, fig.height=3.0>>=
wf_new()
wf_box(2, 2, 44, 92, "", fill = "#FFFFFF")
text(4, 89, "Param pad (p1 x p2)", cex = 0.58, font = 2, col = INK, adj = c(0,0.5))
wf_box(6, 10, 36, 72, "", fill = "#FBFBFD")
for (g in seq(6, 42, by = 6)) { segments(g, 10, g, 82, col = "#E4E6EE"); }
for (g in seq(10, 82, by = 12)) { segments(6, g, 42, g, col = "#E4E6EE"); }
rect(14, 30, 30, 58, col = NA, border = ACC, lwd = 1.4, lty = 2)
wf_note(15, 60.5, "sweep window (N=500)", cex = 0.45, col = ACC)
points(22, 44, pch = 19, col = ACC, cex = 1.1)
wf_note(24, 44, "drag = live lores preview", cex = 0.48, col = ACC)
wf_box(50, 2, 48, 92, "", fill = "#FFFFFF")
text(52, 89, "Lores preview (deployed Lambda, ~sync)", cex = 0.58, font = 2, col = INK, adj = c(0,0.5))
wf_box(54, 14, 40, 68, "", fill = "#16213E")
wf_note(56, 76, "root cloud renders here as you drag", col = "#9fb0d8", cex = 0.5)
wf_note(52, 7, "release -> p1/p2 fields update; double-click -> recenter sweep window", cex = 0.5)
@

\textbf{Cost/benefit.} The expensive part (a fast preview path) is already
deployed; this is wiring and debouncing. Highest delight-per-effort of the
six.

\subsection{E --- Gallery-first Results with a compare tray (1--2 weeks)}

Replace row-list results with a thumbnail grid (the previews exist),
faceted by function, palette, fingerprint family, and date; hover for
metadata, click for detail, and a pinned ``compare tray'' that holds 2--4
artifacts side by side at matched zoom. Derivation actions (autolevels,
repalette, bilevel, DeepZoom) move onto the detail view as buttons they
already are.

<<mockE, fig.width=6.9, fig.height=3.0>>=
wf_new()
wf_tabbar(2, 92, 60, c("Compute","Results","Palette","Render","Favorites","DeepZoom"), active = 2)
wf_box(2, 2, 96, 88, "", fill = "#FBFBFD")
wf_note(4, 86, "filter: [function v] [palette v] [family v] [date v]   search: [littlewood____]", cex = 0.5)
set.seed(7)
xs <- seq(4, 76, by = 12.2)
for (r in 0:2) for (i in seq_along(xs)) {
  y0 <- 56 - r * 17
  wf_box(xs[i], y0, 10.5, 13, "", fill = c("#2A2A3C", "#1F2A44", "#33203A")[1 + (i + r) %% 3])
  if ((i + r) %% 4 == 1) rect(xs[i], y0, xs[i] + 10.5, y0 + 13, border = ACC, lwd = 1.5)
}
wf_note(4, 73.5, "thumbnails stream in from existing previews; accent = in compare tray", cex = 0.48)
wf_box(82, 6, 14, 78, "", fill = "#FFFFFF", border = ACC, lwd = 1.4)
text(89, 80.5, "Compare", cex = 0.52, font = 2, col = INK)
wf_box(84, 62, 10, 14, "A", fill = "#2A2A3C", cex = 0.6, tcol = "white")
wf_box(84, 45, 10, 14, "B", fill = "#1F2A44", cex = 0.6, tcol = "white")
wf_note(83.5, 38, "matched zoom,\nsynced pan", cex = 0.45)
wf_note(83.5, 28, "[diff palettes]\n[open both]", cex = 0.45)
@

\textbf{Cost/benefit.} Pure frontend over the existing
\texttt{/list}-lean-then-\texttt{/detail} flow; turns Results from a
filing cabinet into the place where judgment happens.

\subsection{F --- Studio shell: dockable three-pane layout (2--4 weeks)}

The structural option: program editor left (the B editor), canvas/preview
center, inspector right (the A inspector), jobs rail bottom (C). Tabs
shrink to workspace presets rather than walls between stages. This is the
layout the workflow has been asking for; the four prior pieces become its
panels, which is the argument for building them first.

<<mockF, fig.width=6.9, fig.height=3.4>>=
wf_new()
wf_box(2, 88, 96, 7, "", fill = "#FFFFFF")
wf_note(4, 91.5, "PolyPaint   [workspace: Explore v]   Ctrl-K: command palette", cex = 0.52)
wf_box(2, 28, 30, 58, "", fill = "#FFFFFF")
text(4, 82.5, "Program (text + chips)", cex = 0.52, font = 2, col = INK, adj = c(0,0.5))
wf_note(4, 78, "the B editor docks here", cex = 0.47)
wf_box(4, 30, 26, 44, "", fill = "#1B1B26")
wf_box(34, 28, 38, 58, "", fill = "#16213E")
text(36, 82.5, "Canvas / preview", cex = 0.52, font = 2, col = "white", adj = c(0,0.5))
wf_note(36, 78, "lores while editing; full render on demand", cex = 0.47, col = "#9fb0d8")
wf_box(74, 28, 24, 58, "", fill = "#FFFFFF")
text(76, 82.5, "Inspector", cex = 0.52, font = 2, col = INK, adj = c(0,0.5))
wf_note(76, 78, "selected chip / artifact;\nA's panel docks here", cex = 0.47)
wf_box(76, 40, 20, 30, "", fill = "#F7F7FB")
wf_box(2, 2, 96, 22, "", fill = "#FFFFFF", border = ACC, lwd = 1.2)
text(4, 20.5, "Jobs rail (C)", cex = 0.52, font = 2, col = INK, adj = c(0,0.5))
wf_box(6, 6, 24, 11, "compute 31/40", fill = "#FFF3D6", cex = 0.48)
wf_box(33, 6, 24, 11, "render done", fill = "#DDF2E4", cex = 0.48)
wf_box(60, 6, 24, 11, "palette FAILED", fill = "#FFE3E9", cex = 0.48, border = ACC)
wf_note(35, 25.9, "splitters draggable; panel sizes persist", cex = 0.47)
@

\textbf{Cost/benefit.} The only alternative requiring real layout surgery
in the 20k-line file (or, more honestly, the moment the ES-module split
from D1 pays for itself). Do it after A--D exist as panels.

\paragraph{Considered and not recommended (now).} A node-graph editor
(Blender-style) for the program DSLs: the stack machine is linear with
occasional branches, and the B editor's depth gutter conveys the same
information at a tenth of the cost. Revisit only if programs grow real
DAG structure. Likewise a full framework rewrite (D1-3): nothing in A--F
needs it.

\section{Roadmap}

<<roadmap, results='asis'>>=
r <- data.frame(
  Horizon = c("Now (days)", "Now (days)", "Now (days)",
              "Next (1--2 wks)", "Next (1--2 wks)", "Next (1--2 wks)",
              "Then (1--2 wks)", "Then (1--2 wks)", "Then (2--4 wks)"),
  Item = c("A: command palette + chip inspector (low risk)",
           "tsc --checkJs + ESLint in predeploy (low)",
           "shared-code Lambda layer (low)",
           "C: jobs rail --- poll first, push later (low)",
           "D: XY scrub pad on lores preview (low)",
           "one catalog generates JS/Py/C tables (medium)",
           "B: unified text + chips editor (medium)",
           "E: gallery results + compare tray (low)",
           "F: studio shell on ES modules (high)"),
  Payoff = c("ends tiny-box editing",
             "catches the observed bug classes",
             "kills mid-deploy version skew",
             "work in flight becomes visible",
             "exploration becomes tactile",
             "retires the CR6/CR7 drift class",
             "stack visible; toggle dies",
             "judgment surface for outputs",
             "layout matches the loop")
)
cat("\\begingroup\\small\n")
cat(knitr::kable(r, format = "latex", booktabs = TRUE, align = "lll",
                 linesep = c("", "", "\\addlinespace", "", "", "\\addlinespace", "", "", "")))
cat("\n\\endgroup\n")
@

\section{Closing}

The architecture is coherent: buildless, observable, verified, and frozen
where freezing pays (wire formats) --- the critique is not ``modernize''
but ``stop hand-copying facts'' (one catalog, one shared layer, generated
mirrors). The UI's bones are good and its density is a feature; what it
lacks is the workflow layer --- visible jobs, a real editing surface, and
direct manipulation. Alternatives A, C, and D are small, independent, and
each removes a distinct constraint; B and E complete the loop; F is the
eventual shape once the panels exist. None of it requires touching the
solver, the DSLs' semantics, or the deploy substrate --- which is the
strongest endorsement of the choices made that this review can offer.

\end{document}
